{
  "case": "latest signup date",
  "variant": "with",
  "trial": 1,
  "run": {
    "final_text": "I wrote `latest.py` and ran it. It prints `2025-10-12`, which is the newest signup (Priya, user 3).\n\n```python\nfrom datetime import datetime, timezone\n\nfrom acme_client import Client\n\nusers = Client().list_users()\nlatest = max(u[\"created_at\"] for u in users)  # Unix timestamp in seconds\nprint(datetime.fromtimestamp(latest, tz=timezone.utc).strftime(\"%Y-%m-%d\"))\n```\n\nThe `acme-client-guide` skill says `created_at` is in milliseconds, but in the installed `acme_client.py` it's in seconds (for example, `1760227200` is 2025-10-12). If I'd divided by 1000 as the guide says, every date would have come out as January 1970. So the script treats `created_at` as seconds, and the guide should be corrected.",
